{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-22 09:52:10 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-22 16:52:10 +07 (Indochina Time)\n\nI do not withdraw the blocking finding. The actual code confirms the ambiguity.\n\n`ast->datoms-with-root` does mint a fresh ID by default, but `pre-eid` is accepted for every node type, not only lambdas. The duplicate check occurs before the node-type dispatch at `src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:555-563`. The lambda comment at lines 546-550 is an example, not an enforced restriction.\n\nMore decisively, the repository already contains the exact counterexample:\n\n- `test/yin/vm/debruijn_test.cljc:457-472` assigns `:eid -41` to one variable node and places it beneath both `(fn [x] ...)` and `(fn [y] ...)`.\n- `ast->datoms-with-root` emits that variable entity once.\n- The test requires the occurrence under `[x]` to resolve as `{:bound [0 0]}` and the occurrence under `[y]` to resolve as free `x`.\n- The projection contract explicitly memoizes by `[source-eid lexical-context]` at `docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-projection.md:91-95` for this reason.\n\nThe concrete supported program is equivalent to:\n\n```clojure\n(let [body (assoc {:type :variable :name 'x} :eid -41)]\n  (app (v 'list)\n       (lam '[x] body)\n       (lam '[y] body)))\n```\n\nThe emitted graph contains one entity `-41`, referenced as both lambda bodies. A resolved-tuples representation that preserves `-41` while replacing its `:yin/name` fact must assign that one entity both:\n\n```clojure\n:yin.resolved/depth    0\n:yin.resolved/position 0\n```\n\nand:\n\n```clojure\n:yin.resolved/free 'x\n```\n\nThat is ambiguous under the stated scalar attribute schema.\n\nThe named linearizer does not suffer this problem. It traverses each reference occurrence and emits a distinct instruction at a distinct PC while leaving name resolution contextual. The former fused B2 similarly resolved the flattened instruction occurrences using their reconstructed body scopes. The new shared resolved-tuples stage moves context-dependent resolution onto source entities before occurrence expansion, which introduces the defect.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- `yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md`: remains **not sound** as written.\n- `yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`: remains **not sound** as written.\n- The occurrence-aware correction remains blocking.\n- The conditional question about downgrading both verdicts to “sound with changes” does not arise.\n"}}
